00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:18.000
So if we're going to determine whether theories are ruining movies, let's start with a pretty basic question.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:22.540
Can we prove that theories fundamentally change our experiences when we watch something?

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:26.280
According to psychology the answer is a resounding yes,

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.840
and there are three principles specifically  prove it.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:36.360
The first is known as the mere exposure effect, which states that people tend to prefer things, people, and ideas with which they're familiar.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:43.900
Heres how it works, in 1968 psychologists set up a study where they show people who dont speak chinese a series of chinese characters,

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:53.100
and then asked them to guess what the characters meant. Some of the characters were enlisted only once or twice. While others showed up as much as 25 times,

00:04:53.100 --> 00:05:01.380
and the researchers found that a subject saw the same characters over and over again, they started guessing they meant more and more positive things.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.040
In short, the more familiar something feels, even if it's something as

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:07.160
foreign as an obscured Chinese character, the more likely

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.140
we are to have a positive reaction to it.

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.800
And this absolutely applies to The Last Jedi.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.840
With any Star Wars movie you're gonna get a lot of

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.740
new developments, new characters, new creatures, etc.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.100
